maya: Quick Q — wiring IRSA + KMS for the new `vectorizer` service in staging. We have a CMK in the same account. Batch jobs run in namespace `batch` but need to decrypt embeddings secrets that are reconciled into namespace `vector`. Doing this in Terraform. What's the simplest safe approach to grant decrypt rights to the service account role?

kevin: Short answer: attach Decrypt + GenerateDataKey (and Encrypt if you need encrypt) to the IAM role that IRSA issues for the SA. You can use an `aws_kms_grant` for a minimal grant or add the role as a principal in the key policy if you need conditions.

maya: here's what I tried: 
```hcl
resource "aws_iam_role" "vector_sa_role" {
  name = "vector-staging-sa-role"
  assume_role_policy = data.aws_iam_policy_document.irsa_assume.json
}

resource "aws_kms_grant" "vector_grant" {
  key_id            = aws_kms_key.vector_key.key_id
  grantee_principal = aws_iam_role.vector_sa_role.arn
  operations        = ["Decrypt", "GenerateDataKey"]
}
```
Getting an apply ordering error sometimes (grant references role).

kevin: A few concrete notes:
- `grantee_principal` must be the role ARN (you have that).
- include `GenerateDataKey` + `Decrypt` (secrets-store or external-secrets will often call GenerateDataKey).
- KMS grants are not as durable when you recreate roles/keys — if you expect role rotation, manage access in the key policy instead.
- To avoid ordering issues, either split into two applies or use `depends_on = [aws_iam_role.vector_sa_role]` on the grant.

ellen: For the IRSA trust, make sure the role assume policy is the web-identity template that uses the OIDC provider and `system:serviceaccount:namespace:sa-name` as the `sub` condition. Eg: 
```hcl
data "aws_iam_policy_document" "irsa_assume" {
  statement {
    actions = ["sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"]
    principals {
      type = "Federated"
      identifiers = [module.eks.oidc_provider_arn]
    }
    condition {
      test = "StringEquals"
      variable = "${module.eks.oidc_provider_url}:sub"
      values = ["system:serviceaccount:vector:vector-sa"]
    }
  }
}
```
Also ensure `aud` is allowed (some clusters require `StringLike` on `:aud` = `sts.amazonaws.com`).

charlie: Note on cross-namespace: the SA that the pod uses must be annotated with `eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn` OR you let IRSA create the role via the OIDC mapping (recommended). If you use external-secrets or CSI provider (secrets-store), they will need `kms:Decrypt` and `kms:GenerateDataKey`. If you use a controller that fetches from Secrets Manager/KMS on behalf of namespace A for secrets in namespace B, make sure the controller's role has the right permissions — don't try to grant a pod in `batch` IAM rights to decrypt a CMK scoped by a secret in another ns unless the controller's role is what you intended.

maya: Got it. For ordering: I can add `depends_on` on the grant. Any other TF best practice?

kevin: If you want to avoid grant lifecycle issues: modify the key policy to reference the role ARN (or an IAM principal wildcard for the namespace role name) — that makes access survive role recreation. Example snippet: 
```hcl
resource "aws_kms_key" "vector_key" {
  description = "Vectors CMK - staging"
  policy = data.aws_iam_policy_document.kms_policy.json
}

data "aws_iam_policy_document" "kms_policy" {
  statement {
    sid = "AllowVectorRole"
    principals {
      type = "AWS"
      identifiers = [aws_iam_role.vector_sa_role.arn]
    }
    actions = ["kms:Encrypt","kms:Decrypt","kms:GenerateDataKey"]
    resources = ["*"]
  }
}
```
This is more resilient but requires you can edit the key policy (if CMK is customer-managed you can).

ellen: Also sanity-check which operations your runtime calls. Some SDKs call `GenerateDataKey` then `Decrypt` in different flows. Add both. If using envelope encryption from an external-secrets controller, see controller docs for exact ops.

maya: Thanks — one last question: is it ok to let batch jobs assume the same role as the app pods? Or better separate role per workload?

charlie: Separate roles is cleaner (least privileged). If batch needs broader perms (S3 read for embeddings), keep it separate. If it's identical, you can reuse but be explicit.

infra-bot: PR for this change created: https://github.com/redwood-inference/infra/pull/432 — includes TF role + grant + SA annotation.

maya: sweet, will review the PR and try an apply with `depends_on`. thx! :thumbsup:
